# ThermalBridge engineering rules

## Engineering behavior

- Work in small, reviewable increments.
- Do not refactor unrelated modules while completing a milestone.
- Preserve strict TypeScript (`strict`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`).
- Avoid `any`. Use `unknown` at Zod/IPC boundaries.
- Validate all IPC/native-boundary data.
- Do not introduce a dependency when a small standard-library implementation is clearer.
- Keep `thermal-core` platform-independent. No Electron or Node imports.
- Keep printer transport out of renderer code.
- Never execute arbitrary shell commands based on renderer input.
- Never send binary printer data through string conversions. Jobs stay `Uint8Array` through the pipeline.
- Write tests before modifying bitmap byte-order logic.
- Prefer explicit domain types over primitive argument lists.
- Return typed domain errors.
- Keep vendor compatibility assumptions isolated in printer profiles.
- Do not copy code from vendor binaries.
- Do not bundle vendor drivers (`rastertoX4`, DLLs, OEM installers).
- Use `.js` extensions on all relative TypeScript ESM imports.
- Centralise bitmap encoding assumptions in `BitmapEncoding`. Do not scatter MSB/LSB or polarity flags.

## Electron security

```ts
webPreferences: {
  nodeIntegration: false,
  contextIsolation: true,
  sandbox: true,
}
```

Never expose shell, arbitrary FS reads/writes, or raw bridge commands to the renderer.

## Rust

- `Result<T, BridgeError>` everywhere on transport paths.
- No `unwrap()` on transport paths — use `?` or explicit `map_err`.
- CUPS transport: `#[cfg(any(target_os = "macos", target_os = "linux"))]`.
- Windows spooler: `#[cfg(target_os = "windows")]`.
- TCP, USB, Serial, BLE: cross-platform.
- Never auto-detach a USB kernel driver.

## Definition of done

A task is not complete until lint, typecheck, and unit tests pass; changed behavior has tests; errors are handled; and no debug console spam remains.
